  "prompt": "SQLite integer literal division changes results when transpiled to DuckDB\n\n\ufeffSQLite performs integer division when both operands are integers. A direct SQLite-to-DuckDB transpilation currently produces floating-point division for integer literals unless types are explicitly annotated:\n\n```python\nimport duckdb\nimport sqlite3\nimport sqlglot\n\nsource = \"SELECT 5 / 2\"\ntranslated = sqlglot.transpile(source, read=\"sqlite\", write=\"duckdb\")[0]\nprint(sqlite3.connect(\":memory:\").execute(source).fetchone())  # (2,)\nprint(translated)  # SELECT 5 / NULLIF(2, 0)\nprint(duckdb.connect().execute(translated).fetchone())  # (2.5,)\n```\n\nNegative integer quotients have the same issue. Decimal operands should retain floating-point division, and SQLite division by zero should still return NULL. The type-annotated path covered by #8946 works; this is the unannotated literal path.\n\nReproduced on main at `be837bd` with SQLite and DuckDB. OpenAI Codex assisted with the investigation and reproduction.",
